Should we update the "offline demo" footer? Fine.

Now, since browser is unavailable again, use headless Chrome final test to ensure no JS error after string change (trivial) and to check `Dayananda` answer maybe. We can run a quick harness but we deleted it. Could create a tiny temp harness in /tmp (approved temp dir) not project. We need same-origin? If harness in project can access iframe. Could put test file in /tmp and load via file:// iframe to http://localhost:8123? Cross-origin file→http blocked. Could put a temp file in project and delete. But the only change is a string, so no need. We can instead fetch page and check content versions. The prior full test suffices.

But there's a potential issue: `chatbot.js` was tested when the page title said Dayananda; only changed string. Fine.

Need perhaps test with file:// opening directly, since README says "Open index.html directly". Chat script loaded with relative path; fine. The `app.js` uses localStorage; file protocol localStorage works per file origin in Chrome? Usually yes. Chat uses no modules/fetch, so fine.

Now cleanup: `_mobile-test.html` empty file reappeared from prior session, not ours (timestamp 13:17). We can leave; but earlier we deleted it at 13:14 and it reappeared at 13:17—maybe user. Don't delete. The `careflow` directory also user. Leave.

Update README maybe mention chat in "What's included" enough.

Now maybe add a small "guide" entry in footer? Not necessary.

One more UX thought: The chat's "Register for this" quick reply logic: After recommendation, quick includes "Register for this"; respond text normalized "register for this"; event find null; ALT no; RECOMMEND no; SCHEDULE no; LIST no; FREE no; REGISTER yes; lastPick set and /\b(it|that|this|one)\b/ matches "this" → startRegistration(lastPick). Good.

After `startRegistration`, bot says "Great choice..." quick [], then closes and select. Good.

Now "Show alternatives" when event full: ALT_INTENT includes "alternatives" → alternatives() picks same category or any. Good.

Now "Browse all events" → LIST_INTENT includes "all events" → list. Good.

Now "How do I register?" → REGISTER_INTENT; lastPick maybe set; regex `\b(it|that|this|one)\b`? Text "how do i register" no this → howToRegister. Good. If lastPick and user types "register me" no pronoun → howToRegister; okay. If "register" alone with lastPick → howToRegister rather than register lastPick. Maybe user expects it to register; but the button is clearer. Could handle text exactly "register" / "sign me up" with lastPick → startRegistration. Add? Nice:
```
if (lastPick && /^(register|sign me up|book|reserve)( for (it|this|that))?$/.test(text)) { startRegistration(...) }
```
But not necessary. The quick reply "Register for this" covers. Fine.

Now accessibility nuance: `role="dialog"` non-modal, focus not trapped. When opened, we focus input. When closed, focus returns launcher. Good. But the panel is in DOM when hidden; `hidden` removes accessibility. Good.

Performance: 29KB JS. Fine.

SECURITY: user message inserted via `innerHTML`? In addMessage, we set `message.innerHTML = html` first, then for user we clear and use textContent. There is a brief moment where html contains raw user text, but it's immediately cleared synchronously before being painted; still, any `<img onerror>` would not execute before removal? Setting innerHTML can trigger resource loads immediately? Inline event handlers on inserted tags might fire only on error/load after parsing; removing synchronously likely prevents, but it's bad practice. Better branch: if role user, create p with textContent and skip innerHTML entirely. Let's fix to avoid any XSS possibility:
```js
function addMessage(role, html, quickReplies) {
  const message = document.createElement("div");
  message.className = ...;
  if (role === "user") {
    const paragraph = document.createElement("p");
    paragraph.textContent = html;
    message.appendChild(paragraph);
  } else {
    message.innerHTML = html;
  }
  ...
}
```
Do that. Important security best practice even though input is local. Bump v5.

Also the bot HTML is internal and uses escaped event data and user-derived `matches` escaped. Safe.

Apply.

